       wwggeett__wwcchh gathers a key stroke _w_c_h from the terminal keyboard associated
       with  a  _c_u_r_s_e_s  window  _w_i_n, returning OOKK if a wide character is read,
       KKEEYY__CCOODDEE__YYEESS if a function key is read, and EERRRR  if  no  key  event  is
       available.  nnccuurrsseess(3X) describes the variants of this function.

       When  input  is pending, wwggeett__wwcchh stores an integer identifying the key
       stroke in _w_c_h; for alphanumeric and punctuation keys, this value corre‐
       sponds to the character encoding used by the terminal.  Use of the con‐
       trol key as a modifier often results in a distinct code.  The  behavior
       of other keys depends on whether _w_i_n is in keypad mode; see subsections
       “Keypad Mode” and “Predefined Key Codes” in ggeettcchh(3X).

       If no input is pending, then if the no-delay flag is set in the  window
       (see  nnooddeellaayy(3X)),  the  function returns EERRRR; otherwise, _c_u_r_s_e_s waits
       until the terminal has input.  If ccbbrreeaakk(3X) has been called, this hap‐
       pens  after one character is read.  If nnooccbbrreeaakk(3X) has been called, it
       occurs when the next  newline  is  read.   If  hhaallffddeellaayy(3X)  has  been
       called,  _c_u_r_s_e_s waits until a character is typed or the specified delay
       elapses.

       If eecchhoo(3X) has been called, and the window is not a pad, _c_u_r_s_e_s writes
       _w_c_h to the window (at the cursor position) per the following rules.

